# iconic-panel — RMS + CRM in one app

The RMS and the CRM are **two sections** of one tightly coupled staff app (one codebase, one `useApi()`). Dev decisions A2, A3, A11. The API still enforces that the CRM has no write path to money.

## Structure
```
app/                     the shell: layouts, header (section switch, role switcher, theme toggle, user menu),
                         sidebar frame, login, shared panel components, the section registry
  pages/rms/…            every RMS route lives under /rms
  pages/crm/…            every CRM route lives under /crm
  components/            feature components (by feature, not by section layer)
  navigation/rms.ts      the RMS sidebar tree
  navigation/crm.ts      the CRM sidebar tree
```

## Rules
- **The URL decides the section.** `/rms/…` is RMS, `/crm/…` is CRM. Never store the current section in state that can drift from the route.
- **The header section switch** (RMS ⇄ CRM) swaps the sidebar navigation. It goes to the last page visited in the other section (remembered per browser), or that section's home page.
- **One codebase.** Pages live in `app/pages/rms` and `app/pages/crm`. No local Nuxt layers, no import ban between RMS and CRM.
- **One API client.** All screens call `useApi()`. CRM screens never trigger booking, payment, refund, commission, document or pricing mutations. Cross-section links (deal → booking) are route links only.
- **Each section keeps its own navigation.** RMS and CRM pages keep their routes and the section switch. Both sections use the HILO visual system from the shared layer. The wordmark in the shell is HILO.
- **Access**: each section is gated by a permission (`panel.rms`, `panel.crm`, from Sprint 1). A user with only one section sees no switch and is never routed to the other; a route guard enforces it (the API enforces it again).
